<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bamako</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bamako is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in the south-west of the country. A small trading town when French troops built a fort there in 1883, it became the capital of the colony of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and Niger in 1908 and later of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, linked to the coast by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-niger-railway">Dakar–Niger Railway</a>. It has been Mali's capital since independence in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bamako.jpg" alt="Bamako" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rgaudin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_de_la_libert%C3%A9_-_Bamako.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bamako stands on the floodplain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, near the rapids that divide the upper and middle Niger valleys, in the southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. The city is relatively flat except to the north, where an escarpment, the remains of an extinct volcano, rises. It first grew on the northern bank, and bridges later joined it to the south, where the largest urbanised area now lies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has evidence of settlement since the Palaeolithic. Fertile land in the Niger Valley supported early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> that grew wealthy on trade routes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> and salt. By the 11th century the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> was the first kingdom to dominate the area, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> later succeeded it. The kafu of Bamako was founded around 1650 by the Niare family, perhaps associated with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> man named Bamba Sanogo. It was a client state of the Segou Empire, and when the Scottish explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a> visited in 1806 he estimated its population at 6,000.</p><p>In February 1882 Samory Toure defeated the French at the Battle of Samaya. Some leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> community in Bamako, facing his expanding Wassoulou Empire, began to approach the anti-French alliance. The French commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-borgnis-desbordes">Gustave Borgnis-Desbordes</a> hurried a force to this key point on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and established a fort on 1 February 1883. Samory's brother Kebe Brema led a force against it, and the two sides fought two battles at Woyo Wayanko creek in early April; Kebe Brema won the first but was forced to retreat.</p><p>A railway linking Bamako to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayes">Kayes</a> was built in 1904, and the city began to grow quickly. The line to Dakar was completed in 1923. After independence in April 1960 the city had about 160,000 people. Moussa Traoré took power in a coup and ruled for 23 years. On 22 March 1991 a large protest march in central Bamako was violently suppressed, with estimates of the dead reaching 300, and four days later a military coup deposed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bamako is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and its administrative centre, a separate capital district divided into communes. It holds the University of Bamako, the National Museum of Mali and the Grand Mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bamako” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1754 he defeated the rival Massassi rulers of Kaarta to the north-west and captured their king, Foulakoro, leaving Ségou the dominant power between <b>Bamako</b> and Djenné.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Keïta and Hamani Diori, led their countries to independence; Léopold Sédar Senghor and Félix Houphouët-Boigny sat in the French parliament and served in governments of the Fourth Republic; and the Rassemblement Démocratique Africain, founded at <b>Bamako</b> in 1946, became the first African mass party organised across several territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Commanders such as Gustave Borgnis-Desbordes, Joseph Gallieni and Louis Archinard built forts at Kita in 1881 and <b>Bamako</b> in 1883 and defeated the Toucouleur Empire, Samori Toure's Wassoulou Empire and Kenedougou by 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
